**The page, in order:** sticky header (wordmark, section links, the returns promise as a standing line) → buy section → fabric → sizing → reviews → returns → footer.

**Buy section.** A sticky product figure on the left, built entirely in CSS — a knit-textured panel with a waistband band and a flatlock centre seam — that changes colour with the selected colourway, since there are no photographs. On the right, the panel: name, lede, then colourway → inseam → size, in that order because the length constrains which sizes exist. Colourways are swatch cards with the real colour, the price, and sold-out state shown as a dashed, struck-through, non-selectable card (Oat and Dried Plum). Sizes are a chip grid; each inseam's offered range is printed on its row *and* enforced — picking "24 inch, short" strikes out 2XL and 3XL, and clears an already-chosen size that the length doesn't come in. That's the one behavioural addition; every displayed fact is unchanged, the add-to-bag condition is unchanged, and the bag summary still names colourway, size, inseam, and price.

**Fabric** is five numbered cells in one bordered grid — composition, weight, opacity test, four-way stretch, gusset — each with its own heading so a careful reader can find the one they came for. Headed "Why it does not go sheer," since that's the doubt the page exists to answer.

**Sizing** puts the full eight-row chart (waist, hip, inseam, all in cm) beside a sticky fit finder. The finder's recommendation gets a "Use this selection" button that applies the size and inseam to the buy panel and scrolls back to it; the unsure state stays plain text pointing at the chart. The chart row for the currently selected size is highlighted, so the two halves talk to each other.

**Reviews** keep the height-and-size line as the caption under each name — that's the load-bearing part for this buyer. **Returns** closes with the full statement plus the three terms as pills.

Warm paper background, serif headings against a sans UI, deep-moss accent — restrained enough that the six colourways are the only saturated things on the page. Responsive down to one column at 900px; sticky elements release there.